  "query_preview": "..\n\nLarge scale flow aloft features a subtropical Atlantic Ridge,\na.k.a. Bermuda High off the southeast Atlantic Coast and a broad\nupper level trough over the north central CONUS. This\nconfiguration will maintain a south to southwesterly fetch \nof deep layer moisture advecting northward into the central\nAppalachians through the upcoming weekend and quite possibly all\nof next week. With no sign of any main feature or wave \ntraversing across the area, the forcing mechanism for convection\nwill largely be daytime heating and orographic lift keeping the\ngreatest chances across the mountains and adjacent foothills each\nday. High PWATs in the 1.5 to 2.0 inch range will support \nhydrometeor loading within showers and storms to be efficient \nrain producers and allow for isolated flash flooding potential \nas well. See the hydrology section below for more details.\n\nThe upper trough to our northwest will occasionally steer a \nshort wave across the Ohio Valley, of which may have downstream \nimplications for a round of stronger, potentially somewhat more \norganized storms. Trying to pick this out of the noise is a \ntiming and placement challenge however with predictability too \nlow to highlight with any confidence. In general using a wide \npaint brush to highlight the pops the next several days, with \ngreatest confidence across the mountains where orographic lift \nand differential heating will have best chance to provide low \nlevel lift. As for temperatures, will be going with persistence \nwith highs mainly in the 80s...basically near the seasonal \nnormal for highs and 3 to 5 degrees above normal for the \novernight lows, the elevated dewpoints acting as a floor for \novernight lows.",
